  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 246 PM MDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nLong-term forecast still remains complex and dynamically changing, \nhowever models have at least come into better agreement regarding \nthe onset of the system, though impacts overall appear to be a bit \nless than they did with the last few days' worth of model runs.\n\nTuesday...\n\nUpper low continues to dive south and slowly moving east, heading \nthrough Nevada and towards the Four Corners throughout the day. \nSouthwest winds will increase aloft, which will bring more moisture \ninto the mountains, along with increasingly cold air. High temps \nwill cool off a bit compared to Sun-Mon, but only by a few degrees. \nTuesday evening into Tuesday Night, a cold front will push down over \nthe plains. This will be when the full changeover from rain to snow \nwill occur over our higher mountains, while moisture finally \nincreases over the plains with more widespread showers along and \nbehind the passing front. That being said, latest guidance is coming \nin a bit slower compared to previous runs, so the onset of the front \nand moisture out east may be delayed until Weds morning.\n\nWednesday Onwards...\n\nWednesday is looking much cooler, with highs in the 50s-60s, with \nmore widespread precipitation chances across our CWA behind the \npassing cold front. We may also see the chance for sub-freezing \ntemperatures over the high valleys Weds Night, so take precautions \nfor any exposed plumbing and sensitive vegetation.\n\nModels continue to struggle with the back half of this week. \nInitial, broader trends suggest that the main axis of the upper \ntrough will begin to push east of our area late Thursday into \nFriday, though some outlier model solutions retrograde the low back \nto the southwest and trap it under a blocking pattern for several \ndays. There is a little more consistency shown in this morning's \ndata with a ridge building in once the initial trough has passed us \nby, leading to a slight warmup into this weekend, along with drier \nconditions returning for at least a few days. That being said, with \nso much variation in the solutions, still need to wait and see how \nmodels handle the progression of the upper low over the next couple \nof days.",
